% The `literal-values` switch: literals compared by value (Table 8).
%
% The engines read literals as opaque IRIs (core.rls), so crates/oracle
% reads each literal of the input, and each literal the rules name, with the
% reference evaluator's datatype map, and writes its value to
% literal-values.nt (Codec::literal_facts): `lt <urn:x-turbowl-oracle:value>
% v`, where v names the value, so two literals have one v when they have one
% value. These facts stay out of T.
@import literalValue :- ntriples{resource="literal-values.nt"} .

% dt-eq: every two literals with the same value, each with itself too.
T(?x, owl:sameAs, ?y) :- literalValue(?x, <urn:x-turbowl-oracle:value>, ?v),
    literalValue(?y, <urn:x-turbowl-oracle:value>, ?v) .                          % dt-eq

% dt-diff: `lt1 owl:differentFrom lt2` for every two literals with
% different values. Never listed (docs/specs/v2-1-scoreboard-and-rules.md,
% "dt-diff is never listed"): no rule reads these triples but eq-diff1, and
% eq-rep-s and eq-rep-o, which carry them to the terms equal to their
% literals. So each term holds the values of the literals it is equal to
% (valueOf; equality.rls carries it as eq-rep-s would), and eq-diff1 holds
% for two terms made the same that hold different values: the triple dt-diff
% gives, read where eq-diff1 reads owl:differentFrom.
valueOf(?x, ?v) :- literalValue(?x, <urn:x-turbowl-oracle:value>, ?v) .
eqDiff1(?x, ?y) :- T(?x, owl:sameAs, ?y), valueOf(?x, ?vx), valueOf(?y, ?vy), ?vx != ?vy .  % dt-diff
